---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Long-Term Care">
  <main>

  <section class="card chapter-intro">
    <span class="chapter-intro-eyebrow">Chapter 5 · Long-term care</span>
    <h2>Who pays when someone needs help getting through the day, for years?</h2>
    <p class="desc">Long-term care is help with the ordinary business of living:
      bathing, dressing, eating, moving, remembering, staying safe. Most people
      will need it. Almost no one is covered for it. This chapter shows why the
      current arrangement fails, how other wealthy countries solved it, and what
      a universal, home-first benefit looks like inside this plan.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ PART 1 ============================ -->
  <section class="card ltc-hero">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">Why the current system fails</span>
    <h2>We already spend a fortune, and families still face ruin</h2>
    <p class="desc">The United States does not lack spending on long-term care.
      It lacks a system. Public money arrives only after a family has been
      spent down to near-poverty, the private insurance market has all but
      collapsed, and the gap is filled by unpaid relatives and underpaid aides.
      The result is high cost and low security at the same time.</p>
    <div id="ltc-failure-stats" class="ltc-stats" aria-live="polite"></div>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Spending and payer mix: KFF and Medicaid.gov,
      2022. Care prices: Genworth and CareScout, 2024. Lifetime risk: HHS ASPE,
      2022. Waiting lists: KFF, 2024. Unpaid family care: AARP, Valuing the
      Invaluable, 2021 value. Aide wages and turnover: PHI, 2025. Each figure also
      carries a confidence grade above.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card ltc-medicare">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">The gap most people discover too late</span>
    <h2>Medicare does not pay for long-term custodial care</h2>
    <p class="desc">Medicare covers up to 100 days of skilled care after a
      qualifying hospital stay, then stops. Ongoing help with daily living is
      not a Medicare benefit. Families learn this at the worst possible moment,
      then spend their savings down to about $2,000 in assets to qualify for
      Medicaid, the only public program that pays for this care, and the largest
      single payer of it in the country.</p>
    <div class="ltc-impacts">
      <article>
        <h3>Impoverishment by design</h3>
        <p>To get help, a family first has to lose almost everything it saved.
          The program is means-tested at the point of catastrophe.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Care rationed by waiting</h3>
        <p>About 711,000 people sit on state waiting lists for home and
          community care, waiting roughly 40 months on average (KFF, 2024).</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Cost shifted onto families</h3>
        <p>Thirty-eight million relatives provide unpaid care worth about $600B
          a year (AARP, 2021), often cutting their own hours, pay, and health to
          do it.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>A workforce that cannot hold</h3>
        <p>Aides earn a median of $17.36 an hour; home-care turnover runs near
          75% (PHI, 2025), so the person who knows a client keeps disappearing.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Medicare's 100-day skilled-care limit and the
      roughly $2,000 Medicaid asset test: CMS and Medicaid.gov. Waiting lists:
      KFF, 2024. Unpaid family care: AARP, 2021. Aide pay and turnover: PHI, 2025.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ PART 2 ============================ -->
  <section class="card">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">Systems in other countries that work</span>
    <h2>Every peer country that solved this made the benefit universal</h2>
    <p class="desc">Other wealthy nations treat long-term care as a normal risk
      to be pooled, like retirement or acute illness, not a private misfortune.
      They finance it through a dedicated stream, deliver it at home first, and
      support the family and paid workers who provide it. They spend more public
      money on it than the United States does, and get security in return.</p>
    <div class="ltc-chart-wrap">
      <div id="ltc-gdp-chart"></div>
      <div id="ltc-gdp-legend" class="ltc-legend"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Each bar's tooltip carries two readings. Share of GDP shows
      how much of a whole economy goes to care: the United States commits the
      least, 1.3%, against 2.2% to 4.4% in the countries that cover everyone.
      Dollars per person shows the raw amount, and here the gap narrows, because
      the United States is a wealthy country. It spends about $929 a head, near
      the OECD average, while the top spenders reach two to three times that. So
      the problem is not simply that America spends too few dollars. It is that
      the dollars it does spend are means-tested and rationed, and neither reading
      counts the roughly $600B of care that families provide unpaid.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Long-term care as a share of GDP, 2021: OECD
      Health at a Glance 2023 (total public and private long-term care). Dollars
      per person: that share applied to World Bank GDP per capita, price-adjusted,
      2021. The derivation is set out in the methodology linked below.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">Four models, one lesson</span>
    <h2>How the working systems are actually built</h2>
    <div id="ltc-country-cards" class="ltc-country-cards"></div>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Country designs and dates: OECD Health at a
      Glance 2023; Commonwealth Fund country profiles for Japan and the Buurtzorg
      model; EURAXESS and the German Federal Ministry of Health for
      Pflegeversicherung; Healthcare Denmark and OECD for Nordic reablement.
      Figures are dated to their source year, and the full methodology is linked
      at the foot of this chapter.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card ltc-threads">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">What they have in common</span>
    <h2>The pattern behind every system that works</h2>
    <ul class="ltc-thread-list">
      <li>Coverage is universal and automatic, not a means-tested trapdoor that
        requires going broke first.</li>
      <li>Financing is a dedicated, predictable stream, so the benefit is a
        right rather than an annual budget fight.</li>
      <li>The benefit is home-first by design, because most people want to stay
        home and home care usually costs less than an institution.</li>
      <li>Family caregivers are paid or supported, not treated as free labor.</li>
      <li>One accountable public body plans the capacity and the workforce,
        instead of leaving it to a collapsed private market.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ PART 3 ============================ -->
  <section class="card">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">The plan inside this framework</span>
    <h2>A universal, home-first benefit, run by one accountable authority</h2>
    <p class="desc">The plan carries the same lessons. Long-term care becomes a
      covered benefit for every resident, run by the National Long-Term Care
      Authority, delivered at home wherever possible, with the family caregivers
      and the paid workforce treated as part of the design rather than an
      afterthought.</p>
    <div id="ltc-pillars" class="ltc-pillars"></div>
  </section>

  <section class="card ltc-cost">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">What it costs, and where the cost already lives</span>
    <h2>The largest single benefit expansion, already inside the total</h2>
    <p id="ltc-cost-note" class="desc"></p>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Today's $415B in long-term care spending: KFF
      and Medicaid.gov, 2022. The added cost of a universal benefit is the
      framework's own estimate, anchored to the Congressional Budget Office's
      comprehensive long-term-care option; it is carried inside the plan total and
      inside the new-tax requirement shown on the Tax and Healthcare tabs. The
      confidence grade sits on the figure above.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card ltc-workforce">
    <span class="ltc-kicker">Can we staff it?</span>
    <h2>The binding constraint is the direct-care workforce, not doctors</h2>
    <p class="desc">A home-first benefit rests on one workforce above all: the
      direct-care aides, meaning the home care workers, residential aides, and
      nursing assistants who show up at the door. Without them the benefit is a
      promise on paper. This is already the fastest-growing occupation group in
      the country, and already short, and a universal benefit adds demand on top
      of that.</p>
    <div id="ltc-workforce-fig"></div>
    <p class="note">These bars are headcounts of direct-care workers at three
      moments: how many are employed today, how many the current system already
      needs by 2034 just to keep pace with aging, and how many a universal
      home-first benefit needs once it matures and reaches people now turned
      away. The year 2034 is the end of the latest ten-year federal projection
      window (2024 to 2034), and it is also about eight years into a benefit that
      begins in 2026. A separate, larger figure, roughly 9.7 million, counts every
      hire needed across the whole decade including all the workers who leave and
      must be replaced; that is a flow of job openings, not a headcount at any one
      time, so it is described here rather than drawn as a bar.</p>
    <div class="ltc-workforce-assess">
      <h3>How this squares with the Workforce and immigration plans</h3>
      <p>The Workforce chapter now sizes the direct-care aide workforce as its
        own block, separate from the insurance-transition ledger: about 5.4
        million aides today, roughly 6.2 million needed by 2034 just to keep pace
        with aging, and on the order of 7.5 million once a universal benefit
        matures. Filling those seats and replacing everyone who leaves adds up to
        about 9.7 million total hires over the decade. The plan sets a wage floor
        that lifts aide pay above today's $17.36-an-hour median, and the net-new
        compensation that floor costs is carried in the framework cost model on
        the Healthcare tab, which is what the Tax tab has to raise. The merit
        immigration pathway now lists direct-care roles alongside physicians and
        nurses, and scope-of-practice floors let aides and home-care nurses work
        to their training everywhere. The benefit is funded, and now it is
        staffable.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><b>Sources.</b> Direct-care headcounts, new jobs, total
      openings, wages, and turnover: PHI, Direct Care Workers in the United
      States, Key Facts 2025, drawing on Bureau of Labor Statistics employment
      projections. The maturity figure is the framework's own estimate, derived
      from the plan's covered full-time-equivalent aides; see the methodology.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source">
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/long_term_care_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sources and long-term care methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

    <ChapterNav />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/ltc-client.ts';
</script>
